\title{Spring Freshet Timing on the Alderbeck:\
A Fifteen-Year Station Record}
\author{K.~Aldous and R.~Penner}
\date{June 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Freshet onset dates from three gauging stations on the
Alderbeck were extracted for the fifteen years 2011--2025 and
screened against concurrent temperature and precipitation
records.
Mean onset advanced by nine days over the record, with the
largest advances in the years following warm Februaries.
The three stations move together, and the advance is already
visible in the operating windows of the two flood-storage
basins on the lower river.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The timing of the spring freshet fixes the operating calendar
for flood-storage basins, fish-passage windows, and the first
irrigation diversions of the year.
Freshet timing has shifted earlier across much of the region
over recent decades \cite{karvonen2017}, and small upland
tributaries are reported to be shifting faster than the
regulated main stems \cite{abelson2020}.
The Alderbeck is one such tributary: unregulated in its upper
reaches, gauged at three stations, and feeding two
flood-storage basins whose rule curves still assume the
historical onset distribution.
This note assembles the fifteen-year station record,
quantifies the shift, and discusses its consequences for the
basin operating windows.

\section{Station Record and Methods}
Daily mean discharge from the three stations --- Upper
Alderbeck, Mill Bridge, and Fenwick Weir --- was obtained from
the station archive for 2011--2025.
All three stations recieve the same fortnightly maintenance
visit during the open-water season, and the rating curves are
reviewed annually by the district office \cite{renner2023}.
Freshet onset for each station-year was defined as the first
day on which the seven-day mean discharge exceeded twice the
preceding February mean.
Onset dates were screened against concurrent temperature and
precipitation records from the Fenwick climate station, and
two station-years with instrument gaps were excluded from the
trend calculation.
The screened record is summarised in Figure~\ref{fig:onset},
and the current rating curve for the middle station is shown
in Figure~\ref{fig:rating}.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{4.2cm}\rule{9cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Annual freshet onset dates at the three gauging stations}
\label{fig:onset}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\fbox{\rule{0pt}{4.2cm}\rule{9cm}{0pt}}
\caption{Rating curve for the Alderbeck at Mill Bridge.}
\label{fig:rating}
\end{figure}

\section{Results}
Mean freshet onset advanced by nine days over the fifteen-year
record, from early April at the start of the period to late
March in the most recent years.
The advance was not uniform: years following a warm February
accounted for most of the shift, while cold-February years
showed onset dates indistinguishable from the start of the
record.
The three stations move together, with Mill Bridge lagging
Upper Alderbeck by a consistent two days and Fenwick Weir by
a further day, and no station shows a detectable change in
freshet magnitude over the record.

\section{Conclusions}
Freshet onset on the Alderbeck has advanced by nine days over
fifteen years, the shift is concentrated in years following
warm Februaries, and the operating windows of the two
flood-storage basins should be reviewed against the screened
record rather than the historical distribution.
These trends are consistent with the concurrent records in the provincial hydrological yearbook.

